That explains the constraint completely—and it also reveals one huge catch with the previous shell scripts that we need to fix immediately.
If your remote targets are managed databases that forbid CHANGE REPLICATION SOURCE, they almost certainly forbid REPLICATION_APPLIER / SUPER privileges as well—which means BINLOG '...' base64 playback will fail with Access denied on those targets.
Managed databases only allow standard SQL (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) over a normal client connection. To make this both simple and compatible with locked-down managed targets, you have two much cleaner options:

Option 1: Switch Source to binlog_format = MIXED or STATEMENT (Simplest POSIX Shell)

If you can change your source managed DB's binlog_format parameter from ROW to MIXED (or STATEMENT), mysqlbinlog outputs plain, standard SQL statements (INSERT INTO orders ..., UPDATE users ...) instead of opaque binary BINLOG '...' strings.
• No SUPER or REPLICATION_APPLIER needed on the remote managed targets—just standard INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE permissions.
• No Table_map or table_id tracking needed in awk—you can filter tables with a simple regex match on the SQL statement.

Ultra-Clean POSIX Shell Script (For MIXED / STATEMENT Binlogs)

sh
#!/bin/sh
set -eu

SRC_HOST="://example.com"
SRC_USER="repl_user"
export MYSQL_PWD="repl_password"

# Regex of allowed table names
ALLOW_REGEX="^(INSERT INTO|UPDATE|DELETE FROM|REPLACE INTO)[[:space:]]+\`?(users|orders|payments)\`?"

TARGETS="
://example.com|3306|app_user|pass1|app_db
://example.com|3306|app_user|pass2|app_db
"

STATE_FILE="/var/lib/binlog_sync/checkpoint.state"
BATCH_FILE="/var/tmp/binlog_batch.sql"
INTERVAL=30

while true; do
    sleep "$INTERVAL"
    START_FILE=$(cut -d: -f1 "$STATE_FILE")
    START_POS=$(cut -d: -f2 "$STATE_FILE")

    END_COORD=$(mysql -N -s -h "$SRC_HOST" -u "$SRC_USER" -e "SHOW MASTER STATUS;" | awk 'NR==1 {print $1 ":" $2}')
    END_FILE=$(printf '%s' "$END_COORD" | cut -d: -f1)
    END_POS=$(printf '%s' "$END_COORD" | cut -d: -f2)

    [ "$START_FILE:$START_POS" = "$END_FILE:$END_POS" ] && continue

    # Extract plain SQL and keep only allowed tables + transaction wrappers
    {
        printf 'SET autocommit = 0;\n'
        mysqlbinlog --read-from-remote-server --to-last-log \
            --host="$SRC_HOST" --user="$SRC_USER" --start-position="$START_POS" "$START_FILE" \
        | awk -v cur="$START_FILE" -v ef="$END_FILE" -v ep="$END_POS" -v allow="$ALLOW_REGEX" '
            /^# Rotate to / { match($0, /Rotate to [^ ]+/); cur = substr($0, RSTART+10, RLENGTH-10) }
            /^# at [0-9]+/  { if (cur == ef && ($3+0) >= (ep+0)) exit }
            /^(BEGIN|COMMIT|ROLLBACK|SET TIMESTAMP|USE )/ { print; next }
            $0 ~ allow { print }
        '
        printf 'COMMIT;\n'
    } > "$BATCH_FILE"

    # Fan out to managed targets using standard SQL connections
    FAIL=0; PIDS=""
    for T in $TARGETS; do
        (
            HOST=$(printf '%s' "$T" | cut -d'|' -f1); PORT=$(printf '%s' "$T" | cut -d'|' -f2)
            USER=$(printf '%s' "$T" | cut -d'|' -f3); PASS=$(printf '%s' "$T" | cut -d'|' -f4)
            DB=$(printf '%s' "$T"   | cut -d'|' -f5)
            MYSQL_PWD="$PASS" mysql -h "$HOST" -P "$PORT" -u "$USER" "$DB" < "$BATCH_FILE"
        ) &
        PIDS="$PIDS $!"
    done
    for PID in $PIDS; do wait "$PID" || FAIL=1; done

    if [ "$FAIL" -eq 0 ]; then
        printf '%s:%s\n' "$END_FILE" "$END_POS" > "$STATE_FILE.tmp" && mv "$STATE_FILE.tmp" "$STATE_FILE"
    fi
done
Use code with caution.
(Caveat: Statement-based replication can drift if your app uses non-deterministic queries like INSERT INTO ... SELECT ... LIMIT 1 without ORDER BY.)

Option 2: Keep binlog_format = ROW and Use a Lightweight CDC Binary (Simplest Overall)

If your source must stay on binlog_format=ROW and your targets are locked-down managed DBs, do not parse ROW binlogs in shell. Instead, run a single-binary CDC tool like Maxwell's Daemon or go-mysql-transfer:
• Connects to the source as a standard replication client (ROW format).
• Translates binary row events into standard INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE SQL statements over normal user connections (no SUPER or CHANGE REPLICATION SOURCE required on targets).
• Supports simple config-file table allowlists (include_tables = users, orders, payments) out of the box—zero awk required.
